Gobuster is a computer program—it has no imagination and cannot "invent" words out of thin air. It needs a cheat sheet.

When you pass the flag:
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list.txt

-w stands for "Wordlist". It tells Gobuster: "Hey, use the following file as your cheat sheet."

The path /usr/share/wordlists/... tells Gobuster where on your Linux hard drive that text file is saved.

The Step-by-Step Loop

Once Gobuster has that text file, it reads it like a checklist from top to bottom:

Plaintext
1. Reads Line 1 ("admin"):
   Constructs URL: http://www.onlineshop.thm/admin
   Checks server  --> Found!

2. Reads Line 2 ("login"):
   Constructs URL: http://www.onlineshop.thm/login
   Checks server  --> Found!

3. Reads Line 3 ("images"):
   Constructs URL: http://www.onlineshop.thm/images
   Checks server  --> 404 (Ignored)

...and continues through all 20,000 lines in a matter of seconds.
